  "id": "u10-l01",
  "unit": "u10-culture",
  "title": "Eid",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Bangladesh celebrates two Eids: *Eid-ul-Fitr*, at the end of Ramadan, and *Eid-ul-Adha*, the festival of sacrifice, about two months later. Their dates follow the lunar calendar and move about 11 days earlier each year. Greet with **ঈদ মোবারক!**; the reply is the same.\n\nOn Eid morning people go to the Eid prayer (**ঈদের নামাজ**), wear **নতুন** (new) clothes and visit relatives. At Eid-ul-Fitr almost every home serves **সেমাই** (sweet milky vermicelli); at Eid-ul-Adha, families who can afford it sacrifice an animal and share the meat. Children greet elders with *salam* and get **সালামি** (Eid money): **আপু, সালামি দাও!** 'Apu, give me Eid money!'\n\nEndings: *-e* = 'at', *-er* = 'of':\n\n- **ঈদে আমরা গ্রামে যাই** — 'At Eid we go to the village'\n- **ঈদের দিন সকালে** — 'on the morning of Eid day'\n\nEid is a public holiday (**ছুটি**), and millions leave Dhaka for their home villages.",
